.LP
According to Plato, Anaxagoras was one of the first to propose that whatever there is,
is because that this is the best way.
In
.I Phaedo
Plato lets Socrates tell a story
.QP
.ps 10
.vs 12
One day I heard someone reading,
as he said,
from a book of Anaxagoras,
and saying that it is the Mind that directs and is the cause of everything.
I was delighted with this cause and it seemed to me good,
in a way,
that Mind should be the cause of all.
I thought that if this were so,
the directing Mind would direct everything and arrange each thing in the way that was best.
.[ (
Plato Phaedo Cooper
.], Phaedo 97b-99d)
.LP
This might be one of the very earliest formulations of the idea that things are arranged the way they are because they are due to a process leading to predictable outcomes that are the \f[I]best\f[P] result.
However, this begs three questions:
(i) What is a
.I thing ?
(ii) Who or what is the cause,
the
.I Mind ?
(iii) In what respect is the result the
.I best
one?
.PP
The three questions are seeking answers for what entity there is (i) which is affected by a specific cause (ii)  making the entity optimal or making it performing some function that lead to a good result.
One such answer could be: A variable (i), some natural law determining its development (ii) and a steady-state or an equilibrium condition (iii) leading to a seemingly purposeful result.
.PP
From Plato's description it is clear that Anaxagoras describes the Mind, the
.I Nous,
as the principle that bring order to the cosmic chaos. That is, the cause.
Hence, the thing seems to be cosmos.
Socrates,
on the other hand,
somehow thinks that the good has an existence of its own and that it is the good rather than Nous that makes things best.
.PP
Such teleological explanations seem natural to us humans.
We do search for purpose.
Teleology suits our need for distinguishing between animals whose purpose is to be eaten from animals that might eat us.
Even young children accept teleology.
.[
 Deborah Kelemen 2000
.]
So do scholars of many academic disciplines, including physics.
.[
Kodaj defense of teleological intuitions 2023
.]
.PP
\[oq]That a heart is a heart has a something to do with with pumping blood.\[cq] is the opening line of
.[ 
#
Millikan 1984
.],
who claim that a thing has a proper function which puts it into some biological category.
That is,
the heart is a thing (i) that pumps blood (ii). 
For instance,
molluscs have a body fluid called \f[I]haemolymph\f[P].
.[
hemolymph molluscan
.]
Slugs have two chamber hearts pumping haemolymph containing oxygene and nutrients through the body of any slug,
that is, slugs have circulatory systems just as we mammals.
.PP
Slugs store energy as glycogen and hibernate as juveniles and sometimes as eggs.
To survive the winter they catabolise to sugar,
glucose, which is released into its haemolymph as a cryoprotectant.
This is similar to have antifreeze in the coolant of a car.
The \f[I]Arion rufus\f[P] and \f[I]Arion ater\f[P] occur in Central and Northern Europe, respectively.
Both of them a capable of survive fairly long periods of freezing because of pronounced hyperglycemic response to low temperatures.
The Spanish slug \f[I]Arion vulgaris\f[P] is much less capable releasing glucose into its circulation system.
.[
Slotsbo slugs 2012
.]
